---
model: agent:gpt-5.6-sol:medium,agent:opus:medium
effort: high
budget:
  maxTurns: 40
  timeout: 45m
prompt:
  system: >-
    You are running inside a developer's git working tree at {{workDir}}
    on branch {{branch}}, which is open as pull request #{{number}}{{#if title}} ({{title}}){{/if}}.
    Read the `gavel pr status` snapshot the user pastes, identify the failing
    GitHub Actions jobs and unresolved review comments, and edit files in place
    to fix them. Prefer minimal, targeted edits over rewrites.
    A conflict with the base branch comes first: CI results for a
    conflicting head are stale, so finish rebasing onto the base before any
    CI fix. Rebase — never merge the base in.
    Beyond `git add` and `git rebase --continue`/`--skip` while finishing that
    rebase, do not run any commit-related commands and do not push: each turn's changes
    are committed and pushed for you, after which the PR status is re-polled and
    any remaining failures are fed back to you.
workflow:
  verify:
    maxIterations: 3
    scope: changed
    # --follow is required: the status rollup exits 0 while checks are still
    # in progress, so polling without it green-lights the run before CI has
    # re-run the freshly pushed commit. --fail-fast keeps the loop honest but
    # cheap: it returns at the first definitive failure instead of holding the
    # turn open for every slow scan. That matters because this command declares
    # no timeout of its own, so it is bounded by captain's 10-minute
    # DefaultCmdTimeout — and a CodeQL analysis alone can run for seven.
    #
    # No --actions here on purpose: the agent needs the full red picture, and
    # scoping the gate would let a check outside the selector stay broken while
    # the loop reports green.
    #
    # The PR is named by URL, not resolved from the current branch: under
    # --worktree the agent works on a scratch branch no PR is open for.
    #
    # With a conflict rebase started, sub-second git checks run first so an
    # unfinished rebase fails the turn with what is left as feedback: no rebase
    # still stopped (listing its unmerged paths), the branch on top of the base,
    # no merge commits, and no conflict markers in the rebased commits.
    commands:
{{#if conflicts}}
      - |-
        test ! -d "$(git rev-parse --git-path rebase-merge)" && test ! -d "$(git rev-parse --git-path rebase-apply)" || { echo "the rebase is still in progress; resolve, git add, then git -c core.editor=true rebase --continue. Unmerged:" >&2; git diff --name-only --diff-filter=U >&2; exit 1; }
      - |-
        git merge-base --is-ancestor {{shq baseSha}} HEAD
      - |-
        merges=$(git rev-list --merges {{shq baseSha}}..HEAD); [ -z "$merges" ] || { echo "merge commits on the branch — rebase instead of merging: $merges" >&2; exit 1; }
      - |-
        ! git diff --check {{shq baseSha}}...HEAD | grep 'leftover conflict marker'
{{/if}}
      - gavel pr status {{url}} --follow --fail-fast --logs --tail-logs 40
  commits:
    # A real commit per turn, not the `on: turn` default of fixup: the chain is
    # squashed only at end of run, and these commits are pushed as they are cut.
    - on: turn
      mode: commit
      gates: cheap
---
{{#if conflicts}}
## Merge conflicts — resolve these first

`{{branch}}` conflicts with `{{baseBranch}}`. gavel has already started
`git rebase origin/{{baseBranch}}` (commit `{{baseSha}}`) in this tree, and it
stopped on the first conflicting commit. GitHub's merge conflicts on:

{{#each conflicts}}
- `{{path}}`{{#if kind}} ({{kind}}){{/if}}
{{/each}}

Each time the rebase stops, `git status` lists the conflicted files for that
commit. Resolve every `<<<<<<<` / `=======` / `>>>>>>>` hunk so the commit keeps
its own intent on top of `{{baseBranch}}`, leave no conflict markers behind,
`git add` each resolved file, then run `git -c core.editor=true rebase --continue`.
Use `git rebase --skip` only when a commit became empty because `{{baseBranch}}`
already contains its change. Repeat until the rebase finishes.

Never merge `{{baseBranch}}` into the branch, and do not run `git rebase --abort`
or `git push`: a merge commit fails verification, and the rebased branch is
force-pushed for you once the rebase has finished.

{{/if}}
Fix the failures and unresolved comments visible in this PR status snapshot:

```
{{statusText}}
```
{{#if url}}
The pull request URL is {{url}}.
{{/if}}
{{#if unresolvedComments}}
There are {{unresolvedComments}} unresolved review comments; address each one.
{{/if}}
